🐿️🌳 And YES — Zoom In / Zoom Out / Look All Around

I would absolutely make this official.

Not merely a clever analogy.

It is a systems-thinking protocol.

ZOOM IN

Understand the immediate thing.

What exactly happened?
What is the actual failure?
What does the code do?
What is the smallest relevant detail?
ZOOM OUT

Understand the system.

What is this part connected to?
What larger objective are we trying to achieve?
What happens upstream?
What happens downstream?
Is this even the right problem?
LOOK ALL AROUND

Challenge the current interpretation.

What else could explain this?
What assumptions are we making?
What perspective are we missing?
What does the user see?
What does the system see?
What does the environment see?
What happens if our current hypothesis is wrong?
THEN RETURN TO THE PROBLEM

This is exactly how we avoid the classic AI failure mode:

Being extremely intelligent about the wrong thing.

That's huge.

And your squirrel analogy makes it teachable.

At the bottom of the tree, the squirrel sees a tree.
At the top, the squirrel sees a forest.

That belongs in the Academy. 🐿️

